<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a Right Triangle Using a Leg and an Angle</h2>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A=36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Apply <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times5\\times0.6}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The result names the total flat area enclosed by the three sides: six square units of surface. This is exactly what the Right Triangle Area From Leg and Angle Calculator returns for these inputs.</p>
  <p>The calculator asks for three measurements. Leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> sits next to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the longest side, opposite the right angle; and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the acute angle pinched between them. Because <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} /> equals the ratio of the missing leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> to the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, the formula rebuilds the missing height without measuring it. Multiplying <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} /> forms the enclosing rectangle; dividing by two trims it to the triangle.</p>
  <p>This matters whenever the third side is awkward to measure. A drawing may give the hypotenuse and one leg, while a protractor or an earlier step supplies the angle. Sine closes the gap, so the area follows from what is already known.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the sides and angles -->
<section id="sec-anatomy" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Sides and Angles of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Before the formula makes sense, the labels must be exact. Every symbol below appears in the calculator, and each one carries a single, fixed meaning.</p>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle is a triangle with one interior angle equal to 90 degrees. The two sides forming that angle are the legs, perpendicular to each other; the side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse. Because the three interior angles sum to 180 degrees, the two acute angles satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />, which makes them complementary: knowing one determines the other.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle. It is always the longest side, no matter how the triangle is rotated or drawn. The calculator uses it as side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, so identifying it correctly is the first step: find the right angle, then take the side across from it. A leg can never play this role, however long it looks in a tilted drawing.</p>

  <h3>What Are the Two Legs?</h3>
  <p>The two legs are the sides that meet at the right angle, perpendicular to one another. Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. The calculator takes leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, the one adjacent to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, as its first input. Together the legs form the corner where the 90-degree angle lives.</p>

  <h3>Understanding Angle A</h3>
  <p>Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the acute angle formed by leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. It is the included angle between the calculator's two side inputs, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\angle A=\\text{angle between }b\\text{ and }c]]')} />. This pairing is fixed: wherever the formula mentions <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, it means the corner where the known leg meets the hypotenuse, not the corner at the far end of leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Opposite and Adjacent Sides Relative to Angle A</h3>
  <p>Stand at angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and look across the triangle. The side you face without touching is the opposite side; the side forming the angle alongside the hypotenuse is the adjacent side. These labels follow the reference angle, so switching the reference from <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> swaps which leg is which.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2 text-left">Side</th>
          <th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2 text-left">Relationship to angle A</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">a</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Opposite</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">b</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Adjacent</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Understanding the Included Angle</h3>
  <p>An included angle is the angle formed by two chosen sides. The area formula multiplies <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, so the angle it needs is the one trapped between them: angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. The correct pairing for this calculator is <span set:html={rich('[[b,\\;c,\\;A]]')} />. Using any other angle with the same two sides breaks the pairing the formula assumes, so do not confuse angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> with angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /></p>

