<!-- Section 3: The sine function -->
<section id="sec-sine" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Sine Function in Right Triangle Calculations</h2>
  <p>Sine is a ratio, not a length. For an acute angle, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=\\frac{\\text{Opposite}}{\\text{Hypotenuse}}}]]]')} />. In this triangle that becomes <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}}]]]')} />. The ratio compares the side across from the angle with the longest side, so it is a pure number with no units. For an acute angle its value always lies between 0 and 1, and it is exactly what lets one side stand in for another.</p>

  <h3>How Sine Helps Find the Missing Leg</h3>
  <p>Start from <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]')} />. Multiply both sides by <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=c\\sin(A)}]]]')} />. The hypotenuse scales the ratio back up into a length, and that length is the missing leg. The calculator never asks for <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> because this one rearrangement recovers it from <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. Every later step of the area formula rests on this substitution.</p>

  <h3>Numerical Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=36.87^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\times\\sin(36.87^\\circ)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\times0.6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=3}]]]')} />. The missing leg is 3 units long, which completes the familiar 3-4-5 triangle. One multiplication replaced a separate measurement.</p>

  <h3>How Sine Differs From Cosine and Tangent</h3>
  <p>Cosine uses the adjacent side: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=\\frac{b}{c}]]')} />. Tangent uses the two legs: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{a}{b}]]')} />. Each ratio answers a different question. Sine is the one this calculator needs because the unknown is the opposite leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and the known side is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. When the knowns change, the useful ratio changes with them, but nothing else in this guide leaves the sine family.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Deriving the main area formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Right Triangle Area Formula Use Sine?</h2>
  <p>Start with the standard rule: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{\\text{base}\\times\\text{perpendicular height}}{2}]]')} />. For this triangle the natural base is leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and the perpendicular height is leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, since the legs meet at 90 degrees. That gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />. The calculator does not ask for <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, so the formula must rebuild it. From the sine ratio, <span set:html={rich('[[a=c\\sin(A)]]')} />. Substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{b(c\\sin(A))}{2}]]')} />, therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}}]]]')} />. The division by two survives from the base-height rule; the sine factor is the missing leg in disguise.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Formula Divided by Two?</h3>
  <p>A triangle with a given base and perpendicular height fills exactly half of the parallelogram built on the same base and height. Doubling any triangle along one side completes that parallelogram, so the triangle owns half its area. The division by two is geometry, not a convention, and it applies no matter which formula supplies the base and height.</p>

  <h3>How Does Sine Replace the Missing Height?</h3>
  <p>The perpendicular height in <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> is leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]')} />, multiplying the hypotenuse by the sine stretches the dimensionless ratio back into the length <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. The user measures <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> instead of <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, and the product <span set:html={rich('[[c\\sin(A)]]')} /> performs the conversion silently inside the formula.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Hypotenuse Multiplied by the Adjacent Leg?</h3>
  <p>The general rule for any triangle is that two sides and their included angle determine the area. Here the two known sides are <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and their included angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. The formula multiplies exactly those two sides, then scales by <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} /> and halves. Any other pairing of sides would demand a different included angle.</p>

  <h3>Does This Formula Work for Other Triangles?</h3>
  <p>Yes, in its general form: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{xy\\sin(\\theta)}{2}}]]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} /> are any two sides and <span set:html={rich('[[\\theta]]')} /> is the angle between them. The right-triangle version is that rule with <span set:html={rich('[[x=b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[y=c]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\theta=A]]')} />. The calculator adds one restriction: the three inputs must also satisfy the right-triangle relations, such as <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} />. The general formula computes an area for any included-angle pair; this calculator interprets the result as a right triangle.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('sine-missing-height.svg')} alt="3-4-5 triangle: sin A equals 3 over 5 recovers leg a equals 3, area 6 by two formulas" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">From sine to area: sin A = 3/5 gives a = 3, so K = bc sin A / 2 = 6 matches K = ab/2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area From Leg and Angle Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator needs exactly three inputs, entered in order. The steps below follow the on-screen layout from known measurements to a checked result.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the adjacent leg b.</strong> The leg that forms angle A together with the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the hypotenuse c.</strong> The longest side, opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the included acute angle A.</strong> The angle between leg b and hypotenuse c.</li>
    <li><strong>Use compatible units for both sides.</strong> Metres with metres, feet with feet, never mixed.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter leg b into the first input.</strong> This is the leg adjacent to angle A.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter hypotenuse c into the second input.</strong> It must exceed leg b.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter angle A in degrees.</strong> The calculator expects degrees, not radians.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the angle lies between 0 and 90 degrees.</strong> The endpoints describe no triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify that the hypotenuse is longer than the leg.</strong> If c is not greater than b, a label is wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the three values describe one right triangle.</strong> Confirm b equals c cos(A), at least approximately.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong> The result appears with the substitution shown.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the result.</strong> Read the area and confirm that it looks reasonable.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the solution steps.</strong> They show the formula, the sine value, and the arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the final result uses square units.</strong> Length units squared, matching the inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset before entering another problem.</strong> It clears all three fields for a clean start.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The page offers one calculator configuration: <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. There is no second mode; every area on this page comes from the sine formula above.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Input consistency -->
<section id="sec-consistency" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Leg, Hypotenuse, and Angle Form a Valid Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The three inputs are not independent. In a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=\\frac{b}{c}}]]]')} />, therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=c\\cos(A)}]]]')} />. Fix any two of the three values and the third is already decided. Entering three numbers that disagree does not describe the triangle the formula assumes.</p>

  <h3>Checking the Angle From the Two Side Lengths</h3>
  <p>When <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> are known, the angle is forced: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{b}{c}\\right)]]')} />. Example: <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(4/5)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A\\approx36.87^\\circ}]]]')} />. If a protractor or an earlier calculation reports a different angle for these sides, one of the numbers is wrong. The sides decide; the angle follows.</p>

  <h3>Checking the Side Lengths From the Angle</h3>
  <p>The check runs the other way too. Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} />, the adjacent leg must be <span set:html={rich('[[b=10\\cos(60^\\circ)]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=5}]]]')} />. Entering <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} /> with the same hypotenuse and angle would not describe the same right triangle, because <span set:html={rich('[[10\\cos(60^\\circ)]]')} /> equals 5, not 8. Each angle admits exactly one adjacent-leg length for a fixed hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When the Inputs Are Inconsistent?</h3>
  <p>The general two-sides-and-included-angle formula still returns a number for inconsistent inputs, because it multiplies whatever it receives. But when <span set:html={rich('[[b\\neq c\\cos(A)]]')} />, the measurements do not describe a right triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> as hypotenuse and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> as its adjacent leg. A numerical answer is not proof of valid geometry; the consistency check is.</p>

  <h3>Basic Input Conditions</h3>
  <p>Valid inputs satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[c>b>0]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ<A<90^\\circ]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} />. The first keeps the hypotenuse longest; the second keeps the angle acute; the third ties the three values into one triangle. Check all three before trusting the area.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('input-consistency-check.svg')} alt="Three panels: valid 4, 5, 36.87 degrees; valid 5, 10, 60 degrees; invalid 8, 10, 60 degrees" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Valid inputs satisfy b = c cos A; the third panel shows 10 cos 60 deg = 5 not equal 8.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: Four foundational worked examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Right Triangle Area Using a Leg and Angle</h2>
  <p>Four classic right triangles show the method at increasing sizes. Each example substitutes into the sine formula and then verifies the result with the two-legs formula.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A=36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Step 1: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}]]')} />. Step 2: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times5\\times\\sin(36.87^\\circ)}{2}]]')} />. Step 3: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ)\\approx0.6]]')} />. Step 4: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{20\\times0.6}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\times0.6=3]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times3}{2}=6]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Example 2: 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx22.62^\\circ]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{5}{13}]]')} />. Substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{12\\times13\\times(5/13)}{2}]]')} />. The 13 cancels: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{12\\times5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[a=13\\times(5/13)=5]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{12\\times5}{2}=30]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Example 3: 8-15-17 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=17]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx28.07^\\circ]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{8}{17}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{15\\times17\\times(8/17)}{2}=\\frac{15\\times8}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=60\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[a=8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{15\\times8}{2}=60]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Example 4: 7-24-25 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=24]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=25]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx16.26^\\circ]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{7}{25}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{24\\times25\\times(7/25)}{2}=\\frac{24\\times7}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=84\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[a=7]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{24\\times7}{2}=84]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Foundational Examples Comparison</h3>
