<!-- Section 8: Twelve practical examples -->
<section id="sec-practical" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Right Triangle Area From Leg and Angle</h2>
  <p>Twelve worked situations carry the formula from the page into the workshop, the site, and the studio. Each one names a right-triangular panel, section, profile, or simplified model, works the three calculator inputs through the formula, and states the assumption behind the numbers. Where the real object is curved or irregular, the calculation covers the modelled triangle only.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Triangular Glass Window</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular glass window has <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />. Calculator inputs: <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times5\\times0.6}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This is the flat triangular glass area before frame allowances are subtracted.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Roof Support Panel</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular roof support section has <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ ft}]]')} />. Inputs: <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx22.62^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{5}{13}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{12\\times13\\times(5/13)}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ ft}^2}]]]')} />. The result describes the flat triangular support section, not the whole roof.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Triangular Ramp Side Profile</h3>
  <p>A simplified right-triangular ramp profile has <span set:html={rich('[[a=6\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} />. Inputs: <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{8\\times10\\times0.6}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This is the triangular side profile, not the sloping walking surface.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: Steel Gusset Plate</h3>
  <p>A triangular steel support plate has <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=15\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=17\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Inputs: <span set:html={rich('[[b=15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=17]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx28.07^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{8}{17}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{15\\times17\\times(8/17)}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=60\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />. Area alone does not establish structural strength or load capacity.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Triangular Garden Section</h3>
  <p>A simplified right-triangular garden section has <span set:html={rich('[[a=7\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=24\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=25\\text{ m}]]')} />. Inputs: <span set:html={rich('[[b=24]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=25]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx16.26^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{7}{25}]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{24\\times25\\times(7/25)}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=84\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This helps estimate the area of the triangular section for planting.</p>

