<!-- Section 9: Comparing the sine formula with the legs formula -->
<section id="sec-compare" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Sine-Based Formula Produce the Same Area as the Legs Formula?</h2>
  <p>Start with <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}]]')} /> and recall <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]')} />. Substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{bc(a/c)}{2}]]')} />. The <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> cancels, leaving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />. The sine formula does not invent a new area; it computes the same area from a different set of knowns, with the missing leg smuggled in through the sine ratio.</p>

  <h3>When Is the Sine Formula More Useful?</h3>
  <p>Use it when one leg is measured, the hypotenuse is known, and the included angle is available, while the third side was never measured directly. Field measurements, scaled drawings, and survey data often arrive in exactly this combination. The formula turns those three numbers into an area in one line, with no detour to find the missing leg first.</p>

  <h3>When Is the Legs Formula Simpler?</h3>
  <p>When both perpendicular legs are already known, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> is shorter: no sine, no angle, one multiplication and a halving. Reach for the sine version only when a leg is missing and the angle can supply it.</p>

  <h3>Numerical Comparison</h3>
  <p>On the 3-4-5 triangle, the sine method gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times5\\times0.6}{2}=6]]')} />, and the legs method gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} />. Both routes pass through the same geometry, so they must agree. When they do not, the inputs or the arithmetic are at fault, never the formulas.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: How the included angle affects the area -->
<section id="sec-angle-effect" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Does the Included Angle Affect Right Triangle Area?</h2>
  <p>With the hypotenuse fixed, the angle cannot change freely: the right-triangle relations tie <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> together. Treating that constraint honestly shows exactly how the area rises and falls as <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> moves between 0 and 90 degrees.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Angle A Increases?</h3>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> fixed, <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=c\\sin(A)]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{c^2\\sin(A)\\cos(A)}{2}]]')} />. The double-angle identity gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{c^2\\sin(2A)}{4}}]]]')} />. Area grows as <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> approaches 45 degrees from below, peaks at 45 degrees, then falls toward 90 degrees. Complementary angles, like 30° and 60°, give the same area because <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(2A)]]')} /> is symmetric about 45 degrees.</p>

  <h3>Maximum Area for a Fixed Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>The peak occurs at <span set:html={rich('[[A=45^\\circ]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(2A)=1]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_{\\max}=\\frac{c^2}{4}}]]]')} />. There <span set:html={rich('[[a=b=\\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}]]')} />, so the maximizing triangle is isosceles. A fixed hypotenuse can enclose no more right-triangular area than a quarter of its square, and only the isosceles shape reaches it.</p>

  <h3>Numerical Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, the ceiling is <span set:html={rich('[[K_{\\max}=\\frac{10^2}{4}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K_{\\max}=25}]]]')} />. At <span set:html={rich('[[A=45^\\circ]]')} /> the legs are <span set:html={rich('[[10/\\sqrt{2}\\approx7.07]]')} /> each, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{7.07\\times7.07}{2}\\approx25]]')} />. Note that 90° is not an admissible acute input: it would collapse the triangle into a line.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Alternative tangent-based method -->
<section id="sec-alternative" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Can You Calculate Right Triangle Area Using One Leg and Its Angle?</h2>
  <p>Yes, with tangent instead of sine. If leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> are known, the opposite leg follows from <span set:html={rich('[[a=b\\tan(A)]]')} />. Put that into <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{b(b\\tan(A))}{2}]]')} />, therefore <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{b^2\\tan(A)}{2}}]]]')} />. From the other corner, <span set:html={rich('[[b=a\\tan(B)]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{a^2\\tan(B)}{2}}]]]')} />. These are genuine alternative methods, useful when the hypotenuse is unknown but a leg and its angle are in hand. They are mathematical alternatives, not extra modes in the calculator above, which works only with <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Angle units and measurements -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Angle Units and Measurements in Area Calculations</h2>
  <p>Area inherits its unit from the two lengths multiplied to make it, while the angle contributes no unit at all. Keeping the three inputs in compatible units is what keeps the answer honest.</p>

  <h3>Degrees Versus Radians</h3>
  <p>The calculator expects degrees. Convert with <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Degrees}=\\text{Radians}\\times\\frac{180}{\\pi}]]')} />; for example, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\text{ radians}=30^\\circ]]')} />. A radian-mode angle fed into a degree-mode field silently changes the sine value: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(30^\\circ)=0.5]]')} /> but <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(30\\text{ radians})\\approx-0.99]]')} />, which would wreck the area. Convert first, then enter.</p>

  <h3>Why Sine Does Not Have Units</h3>
  <p>Sine is a ratio of two lengths, so the units cancel: opposite over hypotenuse is a pure number. The square units in the answer come entirely from multiplying <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. That is why the formula's unit bookkeeping is simple: match the two length units, and the area unit follows.</p>

  <h3>Common Measurement Units</h3>
  <p>Metres give square metres, centimetres give square centimetres, feet give square feet, inches give square inches: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{m}\\times\\text{m}=\\text{m}^2]]')} />, and the same pattern holds for every length unit. Mixed units, like centimetres with metres, must be converted to one unit before the formula is applied, or the number has no clean meaning.</p>

  <h3>Why Early Rounding Can Affect the Result</h3>
  <p>Keep full precision through the intermediate steps and round only the final answer. The exact angle behind 36.87° is 36.869897°, whose sine is exactly 0.6; rounding the angle first moves the sine by a hair, and that hair multiplies through <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> into the area. Small on one triangle, noticeable across a whole project.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Area From a Leg and Angle</h2>
  <p>Nearly every wrong answer in this topic traces to a mislabelled input rather than bad arithmetic. The sixteen mistakes below are the ones that appear again and again, each with its cause and its fix.</p>

  <h3>1. Confusing the Hypotenuse With a Perpendicular Leg</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle and the longest side; a leg meets the right angle. Swapping them feeds the wrong length into <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Fix: find the right angle first, then take the side across from it.</p>

  <h3>2. Pairing Angle A With the Wrong Leg</h3>
  <p>Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> sits between leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Pairing it with leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> scrambles the included-angle relationship the formula assumes. Fix: trace the two sides that form the angle you entered.</p>

  <h3>3. Confusing Opposite and Adjacent Sides</h3>
  <p>Opposite means across from the angle; adjacent means touching it, beside the hypotenuse. The labels flip when the reference angle changes. Fix: stand at angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and name each side from there.</p>

  <h3>4. Using Angle B Instead of Angle A Without Changing the Corresponding Side</h3>
  <p>Angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> pairs with sides <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Entering <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> while keeping <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> computes a different quantity. Fix: keep each angle with its own side pairing, or convert <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> first.</p>

  <h3>5. Using Radians When the Calculator Expects Degrees</h3>
  <p>Entering 0.6435 (radians) where 36.87 (degrees) belongs changes <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} /> completely. Fix: check the calculator's unit, convert with <span set:html={rich('[[\\times\\frac{180}{\\pi}]]')} /> if needed, then enter.</p>

  <h3>6. Forgetting to Divide by Two</h3>
  <p>Multiplying <span set:html={rich('[[b\\times c\\times\\sin(A)]]')} /> gives the enclosing parallelogram's area, double the triangle's. Fix: halve the product; the /2 is part of the formula, not optional.</p>

  <h3>7. Forgetting the Sine Function</h3>
  <p>Computing <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{bc}{2}]]')} /> alone treats <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} /> as 1, which holds only at 90°, an impossible acute input. Fix: include <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} />; it carries the missing leg's share.</p>

  <h3>8. Entering a Leg Longer Than the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>No leg can exceed the hypotenuse, so <span set:html={rich('[[c\\leq b]]')} /> means a label is wrong. Fix: re-identify the sides; the longest measurement is <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></p>

  <h3>9. Entering an Angle of 0 Degrees</h3>
  <p>At 0°, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=0]]')} /> and the triangle collapses to a line with zero area. Fix: use a genuine acute angle, strictly between 0° and 90°.</p>

  <h3>10. Entering an Angle of 90 Degrees as an Acute Angle</h3>
  <p>90° is the right angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />, not an acute angle; <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(90^\\circ)=1]]')} /> turns the formula into <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{bc}{2}]]')} />, which is not this triangle's area. Fix: enter the acute angle between <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></p>

  <h3>11. Mixing Different Length Units</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[b=400\\text{ cm}]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} /> multiplies incompatible scales. Fix: convert both to one unit (400 cm = 4 m) before calculating.</p>

  <h3>12. Reporting Area in Linear Units</h3>
  <p>An area of 6 m² is not 6 m. Fix: write the squared unit with the number, every time.</p>

  <h3>13. Rounding the Angle Too Early</h3>
  <p>Rounding 36.869897° to 36.87° before taking the sine shifts the result slightly; rounding to 37° shifts it more. Fix: keep full precision until the final answer.</p>

  <h3>14. Assuming the Three Inputs Can Be Selected Independently</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> are linked by <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} />; choosing all three freely overdetermines the triangle. Fix: derive the third value from the other two instead of inventing it.</p>

  <h3>15. Using an Angle That Is Inconsistent With the Entered Side Lengths</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[b=8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} /> fails the check, since <span set:html={rich('[[10\\cos(60^\\circ)=5\\neq8]]')} />. The formula still returns a number, but it is not this right triangle's area. Fix: run the <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} /> check.</p>

  <h3>16. Assuming a Numerical Calculator Result Always Represents a Valid Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Arithmetic succeeds on any numbers typed in; geometry does not. Fix: verify with the consistency checks and an independent formula before trusting the answer.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A=36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times5\\times0.6}{2}=6]]')} />. Incorrectly entering angle <span set:html={rich('[[B=53.13^\\circ]]')} /> with the same sides: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{4\\times5\\times0.8}{2}=8]]')} />. Angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> is not the included angle between <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />; its proper side pairing is <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. The error is invisible in the arithmetic and obvious in the geometry.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Right Triangle Area Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Seven independent checks, all demonstrated on the 3-4-5 triangle (<span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />). Agreement between different routes is the strongest evidence an answer is right.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Check the Main Formula</h3>
  <p>Recompute <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{4\\times5\\times0.6}{2}=6]]')} />. A second pass catches typos and mis-keyed angles.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Check the Cosine Relationship</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[b=c\\cos(A)]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[5\\times\\cos(36.87^\\circ)=5\\times0.8=4]]')} />. A mismatch means the angle and the sides belong to different triangles.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Calculate the Missing Leg</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a=c\\sin(A)]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[5\\times0.6=3]]')} />. This checks the sine value feeding the area formula.</p>

  <h3>Method 4: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} />. Failure here means the side identification is wrong.</p>

  <h3>Method 5: Verify Using the Two Legs</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} />. Two methods, one triangle, one answer.</p>

  <h3>Method 6: Check Complementary Angles</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ+53.13^\\circ=90^\\circ]]')} />. This catches an angle measured at the wrong corner.</p>

  <h3>Method 7: Check the Maximum Area</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[K\\leq\\frac{c^2}{4}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[6\\leq\\frac{25}{4}=6.25]]')} />. An area above the ceiling is impossible for the given hypotenuse.</p>
</section>

