  <h2>Right Triangle Area From Leg and Angle in Practice</h2>
  <p>The same three inputs appear in places that never mention trigonometry. A glazier measures a window's leg and diagonal and reads the angle off the frame; a roofer knows the support's hypotenuse from the rafter length and the angle from the pitch. In each case the missing perpendicular side is the awkward one to reach with a tape, while the hypotenuse and the angle are right at hand. The sine formula exists for exactly that asymmetry: it converts the accessible measurements into the inaccessible height and then into the area.</p>
