  <h2>Three Inputs Carry the Whole Area</h2>
  <p>One leg, the hypotenuse, and the angle between them turn out to be enough. The sine ratio rebuilds the missing leg, the halving converts the enclosing rectangle into the triangle, and the consistency relations let every input be checked against the others. Nothing in the method is approximate when the inputs are exact.</p>
  <p>The working habit is simple: label the sides from angle A, keep the units matched, confirm b = c cos(A), and verify the answer through the legs formula. With those habits, K = bc sin(A)/2 is as dependable as any area calculation in geometry.</p>
